The assessment is written to session_plan/assessment.md. The commit didn't go through because session_plan/ is in .gitignore. The step allowed for that (|| true), and the file is on disk where the planner reads it.

Top finding: a new security bug, not yet filed. If a cloned repo's .yoyo.toml sets base_url, yoyo sends the user's API key to that address. I tested it on the built binary against a recording stub on 127.0.0.1, so no real outside server was contacted. The config file set only base_url, HOME was empty, and ANTHROPIC_API_KEY held a fake canary value. One yoyo -p hi produced 6 POST /v1/messages requests carrying that canary key, one per retry. There was no warning, no --trust-project was needed, and the startup line never shows the URL.
• Cause: src/cli.rs:1137 reads base_url from the config file with no trust check. Skills, permissions, hooks, notify_command and MCP servers in the same file are all trust-gated.
• How others handle it: Codex ignores openai_base_url and model_provider(s) in a project's own config even when the project is trusted.
• Our own loop: this repo's .yoyo.toml sets provider but no base_url, and no script or workflow passes --trust-project. Gating base_url won't touch the evolve loop; gating provider would need care.
• Not checked: the REPL path, yoyo review, sub-agents and --fallback.

Bugs still live on the built binary:
• #1178: valid TOML spellings [ directories ], directories.deny and "deny" all leave the deny list empty, and the warning calls them typos.
• #1172: model = "" shows as set in config get and the run ignores it.
• #1166 leftover: using the retired github provider is now refused, but the unknown-provider error still lists github as a known provider.

Size gate: two files are one small edit away from a failing cargo test, which reverts the whole task:
• src/context.rs is 2035 lines and isn't on the oversized list; 15 more lines fails.
• src/help.rs is 2946 against a recorded 2856; 10 more lines fails.

